From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: libfstools: look for the jffs2 of a shared volume in the whole volume

The shared overlay volume was taken for a jffs2 only if a jffs2 node was found in
its first 1 MiB. After the vendor firmware used the partition, its erase blocks at
the start were empty (the vendor firmware erased and reused them), no node was
found, the volume was taken for blank and mount_root used an overlay in RAM. The
jffs2 was mounted only at the end of the boot, about 40 s later than usual. The
code that switches to it copies the files of the RAM overlay into .lede over
files of the same name, which could replace settings by those of a first boot; on
the device that was not seen, the settings were intact.

Scan the whole volume, stop at the first node.

--- a/libfstools/mtd.c
+++ b/libfstools/mtd.c
@@ -228,14 +228,17 @@
 
 	/*
 	 * A jffs2 populated by another firmware may start with an empty (0xff)
-	 * erase block, so the first word is not the jffs2 magic. Look further
-	 * so the volume is mounted and never reformatted.
+	 * erase block, so the first word is not the jffs2 magic. Look through
+	 * the whole volume, because the erase blocks at its start can be empty
+	 * after the other firmware or the garbage collection reused them. A
+	 * volume that is taken for empty gets the files of the overlay in RAM,
+	 * the settings of the first boot among them, copied over its own.
 	 */
 	if (fs_shared_overlay() && v->name && !strcmp(v->name, fs_shared_volume())) {
 		static char scan[65536];
-		off_t off;
+		off_t off, end = v->size ? (off_t)v->size : 0x100000;
 
-		for (off = 0; off < 0x100000; off += sizeof(scan)) {
+		for (off = 0; off < end; off += sizeof(scan)) {
 			ssize_t n, i;
 
 			if (lseek(p->fd, off, SEEK_SET) < 0)
